y  the rule armories in the UK as part of  their collecting cultures project where  it sits to this day preserved as one of  the most impactful props in science  fiction movie history beyond its  cinematic origins the popularity of the  Alien franchise meant that the pulse  rifle would go on to find its own life  in the world of video games the weapon  first appeared in 1993 when sega  developed its own light gun arcade  cabinet that provided an alternate  plotline to alien 3 the game was  inventive li title to alien 3 the gun  and cast the player as a Marine  returning to fury 161 the same planet  from aliens to rescue any survivors from  that movie's ill-fated mission  [Music]  fast forward to 1996 however and it was  much easier to wield a pulse rifle from  the comfort of your own home when the  alien trilogy released on PlayStation  Sega Saturn and ms-dos the game was  loosely based on a combination of the  plot from the first three alien movies  and was heavily reminiscent of doom with  much of the gameplay bearing a striking  resemblance to that of its shareware  shooter perhaps the most famous use of  the pulse rifle in videogames though is  in Sega and rebellions first-person  shooter trilogy alien vs. predator which  let players experience the game as the  Xenomorphs themselves a predator or a  pulse rifle wielding Kelowna marine the  original 1999 game in particular did a  great job of bringing a spirit of the  pulse rifle into the virtual world with  rebellion taking particular care with  the details  there was an on-screen ammo counter just  like the one from the rifle itself the  grenade launcher was satisfyingly  explosive and most importantly rebellion  absolutely nailed the iconic sound  effect  sadly the last major game that allowed  players to pick up the pulse rifle  themselves was alien colonial marines  which suffered from a myriad of bugs and  technical issues at launch released in  2013 and developed by Gearbox many Alien  franchise fans were 